    "q-71778": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71778",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5z.$ We can factor out the $5z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z & =0 \\\\ 5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${z}^{2}-7z+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0 \\\\ 5z(z-2)(z-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and $z=5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
