    "q-37508": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37508",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x-2=-2+5x,$ solve for $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x-2 & =-2+5x \\\\ 3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-2 & =-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of ${x}^{2}.$ We can factor out the ${x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
